    "markdown": "# Object-Oriented Programming — Notes\n\n> Structure modular software with classes, objects, encapsulation, and inheritance.\n\n## 1. Overview\n\nObject-Oriented Programming (OOP) organizes code into classes (blueprints) and objects (instances). OOP bundles related data (attributes) and behavior (methods) together.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n### 2.1 Classes and Instances\n\nA class is a blueprint; an instance is a concrete object created from that blueprint.\n\n### 2.2 The __init__ Constructor\n\nInitializes instance attributes when an object is created; self refers to the current instance.\n\n### 2.3 Inheritance & Methods\n\nChild classes inherit attributes and methods from parent classes, enabling code reuse and polymorphism.\n\n## 3. Syntax & Anatomy\n\n```python\nclass BankAccount:\n    def __init__(self, owner: str, balance: float = 0.0):\n        self.owner = owner\n        self.balance = balance\n\n    def deposit(self, amount: float):\n        if amount > 0:\n            self.balance += amount\n            print(f\"Deposited ₹{amount}. New balance: ₹{self.balance}\")\n\n    def withdraw(self, amount: float):\n        if 0 < amount <= self.balance:\n            self.balance -= amount\n            return True\n        print(\"Insufficient funds!\")\n        return False\n\n# Creating and using an instance\naccount = BankAccount(\"Mahesh\", 1000)\naccount.deposit(500)\naccount.withdraw(200)\n```\n\nself must be the first parameter in every instance method. It represents the specific object instance invoking the method.\n\n## 4. Practical Examples\n\n### Inheritance and super()\n\n```python\nclass Employee:\n    def __init__(self, name: str, salary: float):\n        self.name = name\n        self.salary = salary\n\n    def get_details(self):\n        return f\"{self.name} (Salary: ₹{self.salary})\"\n\nclass Developer(Employee):\n    def __init__(self, name: str, salary: float, language: str):\n        super().__init__(name, salary)  # Initialize parent attributes\n        self.language = language\n\n    def get_details(self):\n        return f\"{super().get_details()} - Language: {self.language}\"\n\ndev = Developer(\"Riya\", 75000, \"Python\")\nprint(dev.get_details())\n```\n\nDeveloper inherits from Employee. super().__init__() delegates attribute initialization to the parent class.\n\n### Special Dunder Method (__str__)\n\n```python\nclass Book:\n    def __init__(self, title: str, author: str):\n        self.title = title\n        self.author = author\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return f\"'{self.title}' by {self.author}\"\n\nbook = Book(\"Fluent Python\", \"Luciano Ramalho\")\nprint(book)  # Calls __str__ automatically!\n```\n\nDefining __str__ returns a clean, human-readable string representation whenever print(object) is called.\n\n## 5. Common Pitfalls & Fixes\n\n* **Mistake:** Forgetting self as the first parameter of instance methods: def get_name():\n  * **Fix:** Always write def get_name(self):.\n\n* **Mistake:** Confusing class variables (shared across all instances) with instance variables (unique per object)\n  * **Fix:** Define instance variables inside __init__ using self.var_name = val.\n\n## 6. Quick Cheat Sheet\n\n| Item / Syntax | Usage | Example |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `class Name:` | Defines a new class | `class Car:` |\n| `def __init__(self, ...):` | Constructor method for instance initialization | `def __init__(self, model):` |\n| `self.attr` | Accesses/assigns an instance attribute | `self.speed = 0` |\n| `class Child(Parent):` | Inherits from parent class | `class Dog(Animal):` |\n| `super().__init__()` | Calls parent class method | `super().__init__(name)` |\n| `__str__(self)` | Returns readable string representation | `def __str__(self): return self.name` |\n\n## 7. Key Takeaways\n\n* Classes bundle state (attributes) and behavior (methods) together.\n* self references the active instance and is required as the first method parameter.\n* Use inheritance and super() to reuse and extend functionality cleanly.\n"
